        "title": "Small Town Bakery's 50-Year-Old Sourdough Starter Tells a Story of Family Tradition",
        "summary": "The secret behind Morton's Family Bakery's iconic crust and crumb lies in a living culture of wild yeast carefully tended across half a century of culinary heritage.",
        "imgUrl": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1509440159596-0249088772ff?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80",
        "imgUrl2": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1549931319-a545dcf3bc73?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80",
        "article1": "In the quiet heart of Millbrook, where morning fog still clings to the cobblestone gutters before dawn, Morton's Family Bakery continues an unbroken artisanal ritual that began in the autumn of 1973. The cornerstone of the bakery's renown isn't an automated mixing console or commercial baking enzyme—it is a bubbling earthenware crock containing a living culture of wild yeast and lactobacillus that has been fed daily for over fifty years. 'This sourdough culture is older than my master baker certification,' smiles owner and third-generation baker Patricia Morton, inspecting the spongy, fragrant levain under the glow of the oven lanterns.\n\nThe culture traces its lineage back to Patricia's grandmother, Eleanor Morton, who received a small glass jar of starter from a master panettiere in Genoa during a voyage across northern Italy. Eleanor transported the delicate culture back to the Hudson Valley, nursing it on locally milled stone-ground rye and well water. As Eleanor's rustic sourdough loaves gained a passionate regional following, the culture gradually adapted to the native microflora of Millbrook, giving rise to an unmistakable flavor profile balancing lactic sweetness with a crisp acetic bite.",
        "article2": "Every morning begins at precisely 3:00 AM when the bakery's four-deck masonry hearth oven is stoked. The bakers measure flour, sea salt, and aged starter by hand, eschewing commercial dough conditioners in favor of a 28-hour cold fermentation cycle. 'Industrial bread manufacturing has prioritized speed, shelf-life, and yield over digestibility and flavor complexity,' Patricia explains as she scores batards with a razor-sharp lame. 'When you give flour and water enough time to ferment slowly, the gluten breaks down into easily digestible amino acids, and the crust develops caramelized blistered pearls that no chemical additive can mimic.'\n\nLocals gather at dawn outside the storefront, forming lines that stretch down Elm Street to secure warm country loaves, seeded boules, and walnut-cranberry rings. Despite lucrative acquisition offers from regional supermarket chains looking to mass-market the brand, Patricia remains resolute in preserving the boutique footprint. 'Some treasures cannot be scaled without losing their essence,' she says softly. 'Eleanor isn't just an ingredient in our pantry—she represents fifty years of our family's promise to this valley.'",
        "author": "Emily Chen",
